    passageText: "Decision fatigue refers to the deterioration in the quality of a person's decisions after a long session of decision-making. Psychologists first documented the phenomenon systematically in studies of judges reviewing parole applications, finding that the likelihood of a favourable ruling was significantly higher earlier in the day or immediately after a food break than later in a session. Researchers propose that the brain, much like a muscle, becomes depleted after repeated acts of self-regulation and choice, leading people to either make increasingly impulsive decisions or default to the safest, easiest option available, which in the case of parole boards typically meant denying release. Critics of the theory note that the studies involved a limited number of judges and that scheduling factors, such as more complex cases being reviewed earlier, might account for some of the pattern. Nonetheless, the concept has influenced fields ranging from retail design, where checkout-aisle impulse items exploit depleted willpower, to workplace policy, where some organisations now schedule important meetings earlier in the day.",
