    passageText: "Predicting precisely when a volcano will erupt remains one of the most difficult challenges in earth science, but volcanologists have developed several tools that allow them to identify warning signs well before an eruption occurs. Seismometers detect the small earthquakes caused by magma forcing its way through underground rock, and a marked increase in the frequency of these tremors often signals that an eruption may be approaching. Tiltmeters and GPS sensors measure subtle changes in the shape of a volcano's surface, since rising magma can cause the ground to bulge outward by centimetres before any visible activity appears. Scientists also monitor the gases released from vents, particularly sulphur dioxide, because a rising concentration can indicate that magma is nearing the surface. No single signal reliably predicts an eruption's precise timing, so volcanologists combine data from multiple instruments, and even then, some eruptions occur with little warning, while some periods of heightened activity never lead to an eruption at all.",
