    passageText: "Sociologists use the term social capital to describe the networks of trust, reciprocity, and mutual obligation that develop among people who interact regularly within a community. Neighbourhoods with high social capital tend to show measurable benefits, including lower crime rates, better collective maintenance of shared spaces, and faster informal responses to local problems, such as neighbours organising to report or fix a broken streetlight rather than waiting for official action. Researchers have found that social capital tends to erode in neighbourhoods experiencing rapid population turnover, since trust and familiarity typically take years to build and are disrupted when residents frequently move away. Some studies also link the design of physical spaces to social capital: neighbourhoods with shared courtyards, front porches facing the street, or accessible parks tend to foster more casual interactions between neighbours than those built around private, enclosed backyards. Critics caution that social capital can also have a darker side, as tightly bonded communities sometimes exclude newcomers or resist outside influence, showing that dense social ties do not automatically produce openness or inclusivity.",
